","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/deralmas/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/deralmas/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/deralmas/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/deralmas/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"author_association":"MEMBER","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n- Reproducible in: 4.5.rc2\n\n### System information\n\nGodot v4.5.rc2 - Fedora Linux 42 (KDE Plasma Desktop Edition) on Wayland - Wayland display driver, Multi-window, 1 monitor - OpenGL ES 3 (Compatibility) - virgl (ANGLE (Apple, Apple M3 Max, OpenGL 4.1 Metal - 90.5)) - (12 threads) - 7.71 Gio memory\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhen changing scale size (i.e. from 200% to 100%), the window shrinks down to its non-scale equivalent (instead of scaling up) and the editor still uses the scale size set from launch.\n\n<img width=\"479\" height=\"508\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/afd917cb-95fc-471b-8053-6602a20e77d8\" />\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Set your system scale to 200%\n2. Open the Godot Editor\n3. Notice the scale used by Godot internally (text and icon sizes)\n4. Notice the window width and height\n5. Set your system scale to 100%\n6. See how the scale used by Godot internally didn't change\n7. See how the window width and height are of the same value of previously (without the system scale) instead of taking the equivalent size without the system scale.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nn/a","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/110496/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"time